# ------------------------------------------------------------------ R06-D-08 (Difficult passage, 5 questions)
passage("R06-D-08", T,
    "Anthropic reasoning is a sound correction for observational bias, but it explains the fine tuning of the constants only if a varied ensemble of universes exists, which it cannot itself establish.",
    [
        "Physicists have long remarked that several of the numbers fixing the behaviour of matter seem oddly well suited to the existence of anything as complicated as a living thing. Were certain forces a little stronger or a little weaker, the argument runs, stars would burn out too quickly or never ignite, atoms heavier than hydrogen would be scarce, and chemistry would offer nothing as intricate as a cell. How tightly these numbers are really constrained is debated, and some claims of extreme precision have not survived scrutiny. Enough of the pattern has held up, however, to pose a question: why should the constants of nature fall within the narrow ranges that permit observers to exist at all?",
        "One family of answers appeals to what is usually called anthropic reasoning. Its modest form makes a point that sounds almost too obvious to state: whatever we observe must be compatible with our being there to observe it. We should not be astonished to find ourselves on a planet with liquid water and a tolerable climate, since observers could hardly have arisen anywhere else; to treat our surroundings as a fair sample of the cosmos would be like concluding that a town's residents are unusually fit because everyone one has met there was met at the swimming pool. In this form the principle is not a theory of anything. It is a warning about bias, and as a warning it is both correct and useful.",
        "The difficulty begins when the same reasoning is asked to explain the constants themselves. Consider a lottery. A winner who learns that millions of tickets were sold need suspect nothing: someone had to win, and the winner is simply whoever did. But if only one ticket was ever printed and it won, the winner's existence explains nothing about why that ticket was drawn; it guarantees only that, had it not been, there would be no winner left to wonder. A selection effect does explanatory work only when there are many draws among which selection can operate. Applied to the cosmos, anthropic reasoning dissolves the puzzle of fine tuning only if there are many universes, or many regions of one, in which the constants take different values, and we necessarily find ourselves in one of the hospitable few.",
        "Defenders of the approach reply that such an ensemble is not invented for the purpose. Some cosmological theories, developed to address quite different problems, appear to imply a vast number of regions with differing properties, and if those theories are right, the anthropic explanation comes, so to speak, free of charge. The reply has force, but it shifts the burden rather than lifting it. The explanation is now exactly as good as the theory that supplies the ensemble, and since the other regions are, on most versions, unobservable in principle, the evidence for that theory must come from somewhere else. Anthropic reasoning cannot certify its own premise.",
        "None of this shows that the approach is unscientific, a charge often levelled against it with more heat than precision. Selection effects are a respectable part of reasoning in every empirical science, and ignoring them produces errors as surely as overrating them does. The narrower lesson is that the modest principle and the ambitious one are different claims and should not borrow each other's credibility. The first is nearly beyond dispute and explains almost nothing on its own; the second would explain a great deal, but only on a condition that the principle itself is powerless to establish.",
    ],
    "Difficult", "Dense philosophical argument that separates two versions of one principle, uses an analogy to fix the condition for explanation, and weighs a reply before a qualified conclusion.",
    "R06D-anthropic-principle-fine-tuning", exams=D2)

rcq("R06-D-08-1", "Structure / function", "Easy",
    "The comparison with the swimming pool in paragraph 2 is used to illustrate",
    "how conclusions drawn from a sample selected by the conditions of observation can mislead.",
    [("why living things are more likely to arise on some planets than on others in the same system.",
      "The comparison is about a biased sample of residents, not about where life arises; the planet remark only sets up the point about bias."),
     ("the claim that the constants of nature fall within narrow ranges that permit observers to exist.",
      "That claim belongs to paragraph 1; the pool comparison illustrates the 'warning about bias' of paragraph 2."),
     ("how the ambitious form of anthropic reasoning accounts for the fine tuning of physical constants.",
      "Paragraph 2 concerns only the 'modest form'; the attempt to explain the constants begins in paragraph 3.")],
    "Paragraph 2 says treating our surroundings 'as a fair sample of the cosmos' would be like judging a town's fitness from people met at the pool, and concludes that the principle is 'a warning about bias'.",
    "Linking the comparison to the fine tuning of the constants rather than to the modest principle.",
    "The function is named in the sentences around the comparison.", cognitive="Understanding")

rcq("R06-D-08-2", "Inference", "Medium",
    "It can be inferred from the lottery example that, in the author's view, the existence of observers would explain nothing about the constants if",
    "ours were the only universe there is, with its constants fixed once and for all.",
    [("a great many universes existed whose constants differed from ours, most of them hostile to life.",
      "This is the case in which, according to paragraph 3, anthropic reasoning 'dissolves the puzzle'; it corresponds to millions of tickets sold."),
     ("the other regions of a large ensemble turned out to be unobservable to us in principle.",
      "Paragraph 4 says unobservability means the evidence for the ensemble 'must come from somewhere else', not that the explanation would fail if the ensemble existed."),
     ("the constants permitted observers to exist only within some very narrow range of values.",
      "That narrowness is the puzzle itself, set out in paragraph 1; it is not the condition under which the selection effect fails to explain.")],
    "Paragraph 3: with a single ticket, the winner's existence 'explains nothing about why that ticket was drawn', and a selection effect 'does explanatory work only when there are many draws'. A single universe is the one-ticket case.",
    "Confusing a lack of evidence for an ensemble (paragraph 4) with the absence of an ensemble (paragraph 3).",
    "Requires mapping the analogy onto the cosmic case and keeping apart two distinct difficulties the passage raises.",
    cognitive="Application")

rcq("R06-D-08-3", "Except / not supported", "Medium",
    "The author would agree with each of the following statements EXCEPT",
    "Anthropic reasoning is unscientific, since the many universes it relies on can never be observed.",
    [("Some claims that the constants of nature are set with extreme precision have not held up under examination.",
      "Paragraph 1 says 'some claims of extreme precision have not survived scrutiny', so the author agrees."),
     ("Selection effects belong to sound reasoning in the empirical sciences, and neglecting them leads to error.",
      "Paragraph 5 says selection effects 'are a respectable part of reasoning in every empirical science' and that ignoring them 'produces errors'."),
     ("The modest form of anthropic reasoning is a correct and useful warning about a bias in what we observe.",
      "Paragraph 2 says that 'as a warning it is both correct and useful', so the author agrees.")],
    "Paragraph 5 opens: 'None of this shows that the approach is unscientific, a charge often levelled against it with more heat than precision.' The author grants that the other regions are 'unobservable in principle' on most versions (paragraph 4) but draws only the narrower conclusion that the evidence must come from elsewhere.",
    "Moving from the author's point about unobservable regions to the stronger charge the author explicitly rejects.",
    "The key echoes paragraph 4's language about unobservability, so it can look like the author's own conclusion.",
    cognitive="Analysis")

rcq("R06-D-08-4", "Meaning in context", "Difficult",
    "The statement at the end of paragraph 4 that 'Anthropic reasoning cannot certify its own premise' means that",
    "the varied ensemble on which the ambitious explanation depends needs support from evidence other than the fact that we exist.",
    [("anthropic reasoning is circular and therefore can never form any part of a good scientific explanation of the constants of nature.",
      "Paragraph 4 says the explanation is 'exactly as good as the theory that supplies the ensemble', so it can be good if that theory is; 'never' contradicts this."),
     ("the modest principle cannot be shown to be true, because its truth would depend on the existence of observers.",
      "The statement concerns the ensemble needed by the ambitious version; paragraph 5 calls the modest principle 'nearly beyond dispute'."),
     ("the cosmological theories that imply many regions were devised only in order to rescue anthropic reasoning.",
      "Paragraph 4 reports that these theories were 'developed to address quite different problems', and the author grants that the reply 'has force'.")],
    "Paragraph 3 makes a varied ensemble the premise of the ambitious explanation; paragraph 4 says the explanation is only as good as the theory supplying that ensemble, whose evidence 'must come from somewhere else'. Our existence as observers cannot itself be the evidence that the ensemble exists.",
    "Inflating the limited point into a charge of circularity or attaching it to the modest principle.",
    "Requires linking the premise identified in paragraph 3 to the burden described in paragraph 4; two distractors overextend the claim in different directions.",
    cognitive="Analysis", exams=D2)

rcq("R06-D-08-5", "Main idea / primary purpose", "Difficult",
    "Which of the following best expresses the central argument of the passage?",
    "Reasoning from our existence rightly corrects for bias, but it explains fine tuning only given a varied ensemble that it cannot itself establish.",
    [("Anthropic reasoning explains fine tuning wherever many regions exist, and current cosmological theories have now shown that such regions exist.",
      "Paragraph 4 says the explanation comes free only 'if those theories are right' and that evidence for them 'must come from somewhere else'; 'have now shown' overstates."),
     ("The apparent fine tuning of the constants is largely an illusion, produced by selection effects that physicists have so far failed to take into account.",
      "Paragraph 1 says 'Enough of the pattern has held up' to pose a real question, and the passage never calls fine tuning an illusion."),
     ("The modest form of anthropic reasoning is an empty truism, while the ambitious form is speculation that has no place in science.",
      "Paragraph 2 calls the modest form 'correct and useful', and paragraph 5 denies that the approach is unscientific.")],
    "Paragraph 2 endorses the modest principle as 'a warning about bias'; paragraph 3 shows that explaining the constants needs many universes or regions with different values; paragraph 4 says reasoning from observers 'cannot certify its own premise'; paragraph 5 separates the two claims. The correct answer holds all three elements: the correction for bias, the need for an ensemble, and the inability to establish that ensemble.",
    "Choosing the option about current cosmological theories, which keeps the author's conditional structure but quietly drops the 'if'.",
    "Each distractor matches part of the argument but distorts one element of it: the condition, the reality of fine tuning, or the value of the modest principle.",
    cognitive="Analysis", exams=D2)
